  "query_preview": "../ issued Wednesday / A fairly benign weather pattern\nis expected to continue throughout the next week, with \ntemperatures remaining relatively steady across the region. High \ntemperatures will generally range from the low to mid-60s, with \nlower 70s possible for inland areas late this week and early \nweekend, and no significant warming or cooling trends are \nanticipated. The ridge in the Gulf will continue to maintain a \nstout marine layer along the Gulf Coast, causing widespread \novercast skies due to a saturated and stable surface layer. \nHowever, only occasional drizzle is expected, as there is a lack \nof upper-level support for more efficient precipitation formation.\n\nThe panhandle should remain mostly dry until the weekend and into \nearly next week, as a weakening upper ridge and surface high \npressure allow more energy to pass into the region. While there is a \nshift in coastal flow from northwesterly to southeasterly winds \nbeginning Sunday, we are lacking the strong easterly flow necessary \nto push thunderstorms into our region. That said, light to moderate \nrain showers may impact the southern panhandle and inland border \nregions, from Hyder to Skagway, driven by terrain influences and \npossible convection from western Canada. Looking ahead to the start \nof next week, there is currently low confidence in the placement and \ntotal amount of seasonably heavier precipitation, as model \nguidance remains in disagreement over the track of a weak low in \nthe western Gulf. We will continue to watch this development in \nthe coming days.",
